<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">François Fénelon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1651–1715</span> · Also known as Fénelon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">François Fénelon was a French archbishop, theologian and writer. As tutor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s grandson, the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, he wrote The Adventures of Telemachus, whose criticism of absolute monarchy angered the king. Archbishop of Cambrai from 1695, he was confined to his diocese after his defence of Quietism was condemned by the pope in 1699.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francois-fenelon.jpg" alt="François Fénelon" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Vivien · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fran%C3%A7ois_de_Salignac_de_la_Mothe-F%C3%A9nelon.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
